{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Baltimore Orioles arrived as one of baseball's most anticipated young teams, and they wasted no time proving the hype was real. After a rocky Opening Day stumble — a reviewed home run swinging the win probability nearly nine-tenths of a run against them — the O's caught fire in April, going 18-9 behind clutch contributions from franchise cornerstone Adley Rutschman, whose back-to-back heroics on April 13th and 14th set the tone for what this lineup could do. The team scored 807 runs on the season, with 183 home runs fueling a relentless offensive identity.\n\nMay and June were months of grinding, where the Orioles posted winning but unspectacular records of 16-12 and 13-11. The pitching staff — featuring a 3.89 ERA — held its own, but high-leverage moments cut both ways. Félix Bautista, the dominant closer, occasionally found himself on the wrong end of devastating blasts, including Aaron Judge going deep against him in May and a brutal Kyle Tucker grand slam in August. Yet for every setback, there was a Gunnar Henderson moonshot or an Anthony Santander clutch knock to answer back. Henderson, the electric young shortstop, emerged as one of the game's brightest stars, slugging his way into the franchise record books.\n\nThe summer stretch from July through September was when Baltimore truly separated itself from the American League pack. Going 17-9 in July, 18-9 in August, and 18-10 in September, the Orioles accumulated 101 wins and claimed a division crown. Unlikely heroes like Ryan O'Hearn provided pop off the bench, while late-season dramatics — Aaron Hicks' clutch double, Cedric Mullins' three-run blast, and Adam Frazier's two-run double against Tampa Bay — kept the magic alive deep into September.\n\nOctober, however, told a different story. The Texas Rangers came to Camden Yards for the ALDS as a battle-tested club, and Baltimore's young core couldn't match the moment. Anthony Santander homered, and Ryan Mountcastle delivered in Game 1, but the Rangers' bats — led by Adolis Garcia, Josh Jung, and Leody Taveras — exploited Baltimore's pitching across three games. A season of 101 wins ended with a first-round exit, a painful but ultimately motivating chapter for a franchise clearly on the rise.",
